#: ../../../spi/instantiating-devices.rst:5
msgid "How to instantiate SPI devices"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../spi/instantiating-devices.rst:7
msgid ""
"SPI devices are normally declared statically via device-tree, ACPI, or board "
"files. When the SPI controller is registered, these devices are instantiated "
"automatically by the SPI core. This is the preferred method for any device "
"with a proper kernel driver."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../spi/instantiating-devices.rst:13
msgid "Instantiate from user-space"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../spi/instantiating-devices.rst:15
msgid "In certain cases a SPI device cannot be declared statically:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../spi/instantiating-devices.rst:17
msgid ""
"The ``spidev`` driver, which provides raw userspace access to SPI buses, "
"explicitly rejects the bare ``\"spidev\"`` compatible string in device-tree "
"because spidev is a Linux implementation detail, not a hardware description. "
"Vendor-specific compatible strings for spidev (e.g. ``\"vendor,board-"
"spidev\"``) are also generally not accepted upstream. Device-tree overlays "
"do not help here either, since the spidev driver performs the same "
"compatible check regardless of how the DT node was loaded."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../spi/instantiating-devices.rst:26
msgid ""
"You are developing or testing a SPI device on a development board where the "
"SPI bus is exposed on expansion headers, and the connected device may change "
"frequently."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../spi/instantiating-devices.rst:30
msgid ""
"For these cases, a sysfs interface is provided on each SPI host controller "
"(similar to the I2C ``new_device``/``delete_device`` interface described in "
"Documentation/i2c/instantiating-devices.rst). Two write-only attribute files "
"are created in every SPI host controller directory: ``new_device`` and "
"``delete_device``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../spi/instantiating-devices.rst:36
msgid ""
"File ``new_device`` takes 2 to 4 parameters: the name of the SPI device (a "
"string), the chip select number, and optionally ``max_speed_hz`` and "
"``mode``::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../spi/instantiating-devices.rst:42
msgid ""
"The modalias is set both as the device's ``modalias`` field and as its "
"``driver_override``. This ensures that the device binds to the named driver "
"directly, bypassing the normal bus matching logic (OF, ACPI, and "
"``id_table``). This is necessary because drivers like ``spidev`` "
"deliberately exclude generic names from their ``id_table``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../spi/instantiating-devices.rst:48
msgid ""
"If ``max_speed_hz`` is omitted or 0, ``spi_setup()`` clamps it to the "
"controller's maximum speed. If ``mode`` is omitted, SPI mode 0 (CPOL=0, "
"CPHA=0) is used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../spi/instantiating-devices.rst:52
msgid ""
"File ``delete_device`` takes a single parameter: the chip select number. As "
"no two devices can share a chip select on a given SPI bus, the chip select "
"is sufficient to uniquely identify the device."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../spi/instantiating-devices.rst:56
msgid "Examples::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../spi/instantiating-devices.rst:67
msgid ""
"The attributes are added after the host controller and its firmware-"
"described devices have been registered. Their addition emits a ``change`` "
"uevent, allowing a udev rule to write to ``new_device`` when the interface "
"is ready."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../spi/instantiating-devices.rst:72
msgid "Limitations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../spi/instantiating-devices.rst:74
msgid ""
"Devices created through this interface have the following limitations "
"compared to devices declared via device-tree:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../spi/instantiating-devices.rst:77
msgid "No interrupt (IRQ) support."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../spi/instantiating-devices.rst:78
msgid ""
"No additional properties such as ``spi-max-frequency`` DT bindings or "
"controller-specific configuration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../spi/instantiating-devices.rst:80
msgid "No platform data or software nodes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../spi/instantiating-devices.rst:82
msgid ""
"For ``spidev`` usage these limitations are not relevant, since spidev "
"provides a raw byte-level interface that does not require any of these "
"features."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../spi/instantiating-devices.rst:86
msgid ""
"Only devices created via ``new_device`` can be removed through "
"``delete_device``. Devices declared via device-tree, ACPI, or board files "
"are not affected by this interface."
msgstr ""
